1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,550
in recent years, the AI field has shown clearly

2
00:00:02,550 --> 00:00:05,600
how piling up concepts can mask weak technical prowess

3
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:07,116
Lately, a hot new term '

4
00:00:07,116 --> 00:00:10,400
'Skills' has emerged once more—what is its core meaning?

5
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,869
How do they link with Workflow, Prompt, MCP, and Command?

6
00:00:13,869 --> 00:00:17,659
Next, we'll tie all these concepts together and clarify them.

7
00:00:17,660 --> 00:00:18,455
Did you like it?

8
00:00:18,455 --> 00:00:19,499
Did you follow before?

9
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:19,500
Continue

10
00:00:20,860 --> 00:00:23,149
We know that to get AI to work,

11
00:00:23,149 --> 00:00:27,500
the simplest way is to give it a command — a prompt phrase.

12
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:29,015
like 'Write me a resume,'

13
00:00:29,015 --> 00:00:30,509
but what the AI spits out

14
00:00:30,509 --> 00:00:33,439
may fall short in format and content as we hoped

15
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:34,700
to improve results,

16
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:37,643
you might add many rules — like centering personal info,

17
00:00:37,643 --> 00:00:39,134
listing education in tables,

18
00:00:39,134 --> 00:00:41,840
and breaking down projects using the STAR method.

19
00:00:42,170 --> 00:00:45,104
such detailed-step prompts are called Structured Prompts

20
00:00:45,104 --> 00:00:45,890
in our context.

21
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:47,740
What is Command?

22
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:51,040
But typing such long prompts each time isn't practical.

23
00:00:51,380 --> 00:00:53,631
so many AI clients, like claude code,

24
00:00:53,631 --> 00:00:55,974
let you save common prompts as files,

25
00:00:55,974 --> 00:00:59,100
which you can summon via shortcut-like commands.

26
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:01,808
this 'replace fixed text with a short command'

27
00:01:01,808 --> 00:01:03,080
feature is called Command.

28
00:01:04,290 --> 00:01:05,272
What is a System

29
00:01:05,272 --> 00:01:08,648
Prompt? Commands let us input long prompts more easily,

30
00:01:08,648 --> 00:01:11,779
but as prompts grow longer, AI becomes less obedient.

31
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:13,176
tell it to use tables,

32
00:01:13,176 --> 00:01:14,227
it gives plain text;

33
00:01:14,227 --> 00:01:15,699
ask for centering, it ignores.

34
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:16,266
Is there a way to make it obey? Yes! What we type in the input box is called 'User Prompt' in the LLM domain.

35
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:27,380
there is a higher-tier command known as the system prompt

36
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:30,630
In general, the same request in a system prompt

37
00:01:30,630 --> 00:01:32,332
yields better instruction-

38
00:01:32,332 --> 00:01:34,820
-following from LLMs than User Prompts.

39
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:38,149
but when we use AI daily, there's just one input box —

40
00:01:38,149 --> 00:01:41,459
whatever we type in is sent as a User Prompt to the LLM

41
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:44,500
system prompts are sent by the AI client in the back.

42
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:46,902
How can we send input text as a system

43
00:01:46,902 --> 00:01:48,300
prompt to the LLM instead?

44
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:50,583
some AI tools have a dedicated slot

45
00:01:50,583 --> 00:01:53,496
where we can use files to store system prompts

46
00:01:53,496 --> 00:01:54,406
we want to send,

47
00:01:54,406 --> 00:01:57,379
like cursor's cursor.ru or cloud code's cloud.md.

48
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:00,694
The most common one could be 'Please reply in Chinese.'

49
00:02:00,694 --> 00:02:01,259
Enjoy that?

50
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:03,100
A Triple Combo click is fine?

51
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:06,967
What is Metadata? In daily tasks we face many scenarios,

52
00:02:06,967 --> 00:02:08,156
like crafting resumes,

53
00:02:08,156 --> 00:02:10,990
weekly reports, or emails—each with distinct needs.

54
00:02:11,230 --> 00:02:13,257
stuffing them all into one system

55
00:02:13,257 --> 00:02:15,470
prompt file just doesn't make sense

56
00:02:15,750 --> 00:02:18,892
The simplest way is to split them into separate MD files

57
00:02:18,892 --> 00:02:19,510
by context.

58
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:22,860
So the question is, how does AI know which file to use?

59
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:24,636
feeding all files to the LLM,

60
00:02:24,636 --> 00:02:27,181
reading them then deciding—this isn't feasible,

61
00:02:27,181 --> 00:02:29,240
since files turn to tokens as numbers

62
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:32,400
More files mean more tokens, which gets costly—what now?

63
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:35,921
we can add a brief description at the start of each file,

64
00:02:35,921 --> 00:02:38,011
clearly stating what the file is for

65
00:02:38,011 --> 00:02:39,580
and when it should be used.

66
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:42,487
This kind of hint data is called metadata.

67
00:02:42,487 --> 00:02:45,281
So, when a user sends 'Help me write a resume'

68
00:02:45,281 --> 00:02:46,361
in the AI client,

69
00:02:46,361 --> 00:02:49,918
the client only needs to send this metadata to the model.

70
00:02:50,140 --> 00:02:52,820
Since Metadata is small, it doesn't cost many tokens.

71
00:02:53,090 --> 00:02:55,111
the model detects this is a resume scenario,

72
00:02:55,111 --> 00:02:56,249
returns to the AI client,

73
00:02:56,249 --> 00:02:58,505
which then loads the full resume expert content

74
00:02:58,505 --> 00:03:00,329
and sends the system prompt to the AI.

75
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:02,822
AI uses the prompt to generate the resume,

76
00:03:02,822 --> 00:03:03,521
then sends it

77
00:03:03,521 --> 00:03:06,213
back to the client—this preserves prompt efficacy

78
00:03:06,213 --> 00:03:07,560
while saving many tokens.

79
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:08,923
If you're still awake,

80
00:03:08,923 --> 00:03:11,549
drop a 0 in danmu so I can see how many are left.

81
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:15,351
What are Reference and Script? As needs grow finer,

82
00:03:15,351 --> 00:03:17,770
single files can still become quite large.

83
00:03:17,970 --> 00:03:18,566
for resumes,

84
00:03:18,566 --> 00:03:21,441
different roles need varied approaches—product roles

85
00:03:21,441 --> 00:03:22,798
stress business insight,

86
00:03:22,798 --> 00:03:24,804
dev roles highlight engineering depth,

87
00:03:24,804 --> 00:03:27,030
and AI roles focus on paper achievements

88
00:03:27,330 --> 00:03:27,770
What to do?

89
00:03:27,890 --> 00:03:29,746
Keep splitting: in 'write-resume.md',

90
00:03:29,746 --> 00:03:31,329
we can add a role entry point,

91
00:03:31,329 --> 00:03:33,677
routing different roles to separate files.

92
00:03:33,677 --> 00:03:35,860
If a single role file is still too large,

93
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:36,789
split it further.

94
00:03:37,060 --> 00:03:39,888
the AI client would then use 'cat' and such sys cmds

95
00:03:39,888 --> 00:03:41,302
to read the file outline,

96
00:03:41,302 --> 00:03:42,660
then drill down by role

97
00:03:42,850 --> 00:03:44,584
just read the few files you need;

98
00:03:44,584 --> 00:03:46,970
unused ones won't consume any tokens at all.

99
00:03:47,190 --> 00:03:48,974
this on-demand data read method

100
00:03:48,974 --> 00:03:50,990
is known as progressive disclosure.

101
00:03:51,430 --> 00:03:54,537
these split-out files can serve as reference materials

102
00:03:54,537 --> 00:03:57,070
and be placed in a folder named 'references'.

103
00:03:57,240 --> 00:03:59,103
As the name suggests, if the LLM needs it,

104
00:03:59,103 --> 00:04:00,640
it can reference those materials.

105
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:03,320
If we can run system commands to read files,

106
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:05,280
then we should be able to run code too.

107
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:08,453
then write a Python script to save text into a Word doc

108
00:04:08,453 --> 00:04:10,480
and export it as PDF — that makes sense.

109
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:13,592
put these code files into a folder named 'Scripts',

110
00:04:13,592 --> 00:04:14,602
and in the prompt,

111
00:04:14,602 --> 00:04:17,279
specify which script to run in each scenario

112
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:19,779
this way, the LLM can work with the AI client

113
00:04:19,779 --> 00:04:20,639
via code scripts

114
00:04:20,639 --> 00:04:22,760
to enable features beyond plain text chat

115
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:24,056
If you've made it here,

116
00:04:24,056 --> 00:04:25,924
you've outpaced 99% of viewers—drop a '1'

117
00:04:25,924 --> 00:04:27,580
so I can see how many there are

118
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:29,520
What is Skill?

119
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:33,658
Here, we've turned our commonly used User Prompts into system

120
00:04:33,658 --> 00:04:35,840
prompts stored as individual files.

121
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,081
then use Metadata and split files

122
00:04:38,081 --> 00:04:40,479
to load data by conditions and scenes,

123
00:04:40,479 --> 00:04:42,120
cutting token use sharply

124
00:04:42,270 --> 00:04:44,519
and place any reference materials and code

125
00:04:44,519 --> 00:04:45,966
scripts used by the prompt

126
00:04:45,966 --> 00:04:48,163
into the References and Scripts folders —

127
00:04:48,163 --> 00:04:50,359
one for reading docs, one for running code.

128
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:53,372
rename the main prompt file to 'skill.md',

129
00:04:53,372 --> 00:04:55,626
then bundle them all into a folder

130
00:04:55,626 --> 00:04:58,676
with a name—like 'resume_writer.**' for resumes

131
00:04:58,676 --> 00:05:00,599
or 'article-writer' for essays.

132
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:02,053
this system prompt,

133
00:05:02,053 --> 00:05:04,899
externalized as a folder with dynamic loading,

134
00:05:04,899 --> 00:05:06,199
is known as Skill.**.

135
00:05:06,199 --> 00:05:08,860
Let's look at its full workflow in .** form.

136
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:12,088
First, place it in the 'skills' folder of Claude Code —

137
00:05:12,088 --> 00:05:14,034
that completes the installation.

138
00:05:14,034 --> 00:05:16,569
Now, Claude Code will recognize its commands.

139
00:05:16,920 --> 00:05:19,093
then we, as usual, send in the chat box

140
00:05:19,093 --> 00:05:20,680
what we want to accomplish.

141
00:05:20,880 --> 00:05:23,395
e.g., 'write a resume PDF' — Cloud Code

142
00:05:23,395 --> 00:05:27,970
loads local skill files and sends their metadata to the LLM.

143
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:30,726
the LLM identifies which skill is needed

144
00:05:30,726 --> 00:05:31,980
and tells cloud code.

145
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:34,834
cloud code loads the matching skill file into the system

146
00:05:34,834 --> 00:05:36,260
prompt and sends it to the LLM.

147
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:39,501
the LLM directs cloud code to read relevant files,

148
00:05:39,501 --> 00:05:42,655
possibly multiple sources, or even run local scripts

149
00:05:42,655 --> 00:05:45,810
to generate a PDF and return the output to the LLM.

150
00:05:46,040 --> 00:05:48,886
The LLM then outputs the full result to the user,

151
00:05:48,886 --> 00:05:51,595
completing the flow—so what sets Skills apart

152
00:05:51,595 --> 00:05:52,920
from MCP and Workflow?

153
00:05:53,780 --> 00:05:56,706
We know the difference: LLM is like the brain,

154
00:05:56,706 --> 00:05:59,300
and MCP lets it control external tools

155
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:00,745
the big minds added MCP,

156
00:06:00,745 --> 00:06:03,000
which acts like hands for the main brain

157
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:04,950
MCP Plugins are the tools in hand.

158
00:06:04,950 --> 00:06:06,446
But giving a college student

159
00:06:06,446 --> 00:06:07,312
a bunch of tools

160
00:06:07,312 --> 00:06:10,510
won't let him fix a car—experience and process are missing,

161
00:06:10,510 --> 00:06:11,900
hence the need for Skills.

162
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:14,731
you can think of it as operational know-how—guiding

163
00:06:14,731 --> 00:06:15,900
which tools to combine,

164
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:17,780
in what order, for specific scenarios

165
00:06:18,090 --> 00:06:20,008
note that the tools mentioned here

166
00:06:20,008 --> 00:06:22,547
could be MCP Plugins or local script scripts;

167
00:06:22,547 --> 00:06:24,692
Skills look like orchestration tools,

168
00:06:24,692 --> 00:06:26,779
but how do they differ from Workflows?

169
00:06:27,810 --> 00:06:29,947
The difference between Skill and Workflow:

170
00:06:29,947 --> 00:06:31,876
many tasks can be broken into steps.

171
00:06:31,876 --> 00:06:34,482
For example, making a video involves picking topics,

172
00:06:34,482 --> 00:06:36,619
writing scripts, and creating storyboards.

173
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:38,784
to address such process-driven needs,

174
00:06:38,784 --> 00:06:41,859
many developers have open-sourced low-code tools like n8n,

175
00:06:41,859 --> 00:06:44,450
which let you build pipelines via drag-and-drop.

176
00:06:44,850 --> 00:06:47,421
a process where steps are arranged and orchestrated

177
00:06:47,421 --> 00:06:48,531
via rule-based setups

178
00:06:48,531 --> 00:06:49,490
is called Workflow

179
00:06:49,670 --> 00:06:52,183
skills are also about 'logic orchestration,'

180
00:06:52,183 --> 00:06:53,428
but unlike Workflows,

181
00:06:53,428 --> 00:06:56,230
whose flow structure is fixed at design time

182
00:06:56,230 --> 00:06:58,441
and Skills' execution is LLM-driven,

183
00:06:58,441 --> 00:07:01,415
offering greater flexibility—both can achieve

184
00:07:01,415 --> 00:07:02,540
similar outcomes

185
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:04,042
So, 'loosely speaking,'

186
00:07:04,042 --> 00:07:07,580
a Skill is just an 'LLM-driven Workflow' in simple terms.

187
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:08,920
Are folks all clear now?

188
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:11,076
Alright, if you found this video helpful,

189
00:07:11,076 --> 00:07:13,387
be sure to share it with your not-so-bright bro;

190
00:07:13,387 --> 00:07:14,880
text notes are in the comments.

191
